These incorrect options center on classical communication tasks unrelated to quantum communication’s core focus: secure, quantum-enabled data handling, not unencrypted transfer or broadcast frequency management.

6. What is photon polarization?
a) Photon charge;
b) Photon speed;
c) Orientation of the electromagnetic field strength vector;
d) The distance a photon travels before being absorbed by a detector
Wrong options confuse polarization with unrelated photon properties: photons have no electric charge, their speed is constant in a vacuum, and travel distance depends on environmental factors, not polarization state.

9. What bases are most often used in QKD protocols?
a) Vertical-horizontal and diagonal (0°-90° and 45°-135°);
b) Circular (clockwise/counterclockwise) and longitudinal (parallel to photon travel direction)
c) Vertical-circular and mixed elliptical (0° and irregular elliptical angles)
d) Horizontal-oblique (30°-120°) and perpendicular-only (90° single axis)
Incorrect bases either rely on impractical orientations (longitudinal polarization can’t be measured via standard QKD detectors) or combine incompatible states that fail to support reliable secure key exchange.

10. What happens if the recipient measures the polarization of the photon in the wrong basis?
a) With a probability of 0.5 the bit will be measured correctly;
b) Will always be measured correctly;
c) Will always be measured incorrectly;
d) The photon’s polarization state will permanently collapse, making all subsequent data unreadable
Wrong options misframe quantum measurement outcomes; permanent collapse disabling all future data is not a standard result—mismatched bases only cause probabilistic error for that specific bit, not total data loss.

